WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.630
Faults, joints, folds, tilted layers, and shear zones can form in

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.570
related systems. A fault propagating upward can bend layers above it,

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.150
and slip over a bend in a thrust fault can fold

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:16.750
the overlying rocks. Structural associations must be supported by geometry.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.970
Compression can move thrust sheets over one another and shorten a

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:24.830
region. Beds in and above the sheets may form anticlines and

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.570
synclines. Related joints, smaller faults, and metamorphic fabrics can occur, but

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.350
not every nearby structure must share the same age.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.770
Cleavage in a fold commonly lies roughly parallel to the axial

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.250
surface. It cuts bedding at different angles on the limbs and

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.110
may be nearly perpendicular to bedding near a hinge in the

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.330
supplied example. Trace both surfaces across the fold.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.430
A younger fault can cut an older fold, and an older

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.910
fault can itself be folded.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:56.330
Use cross-cutting, overprinting, and the geometry of displaced markers to order

00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:00.150
events. Proximity alone does not prove two structures formed together.
